Z||000000||camaro2fiero||05-24-2001||10:00 PM||||If the WS6 is performance package suspension. What is WS8 stock suspension?||||reg||1
Z||000001||AkursedX||05-24-2001||10:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by camaro2fiero:<BR><B>If the WS6 is performance package suspension. What is WS8 stock suspension?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Not sure on this exactly, but i think the WS8 would be different, lighter, spring rates, and it is lacking a rear swaybay<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||camaro2fiero||05-24-2001||10:09 PM||||This was the RPO code on my 84 2m4.Thank you for the reply.New to the Fiero world and trying to figure it out,Thanks Again||||reg||1
Z||000003||Pontiaddict||05-24-2001||10:37 PM||||If you plan on getting lowering springs and a set of aftermarket swaybars, then you have a better suspension than a WS6. If someone is using it as a selling point and you're looking for a project car, then don't worry about it. If you want a good handling car without modifyng anything (for those "keep it stock," folks.) then it's a bonus.<P>* not that this is your situation, but someone else might read this and find it helpful.||||reg||1
Z||000004||camaro2fiero||05-24-2001||11:19 PM||||Thank you Pontiaddict, So are you saying it is better than stock? So if that is true and I do some mods it will be better,cool.I plan on doing some mods but I still need to figure what engine to put in .So I can build suspension around that.Thanks for the replys,this forum is awesome.||||reg||1
Z||000005||AkursedX||05-25-2001||10:56 AM||||If you're going to do it piece by piece, I would recommend getting the rear swaybar 1st, that will be your most noticeable imporvement.  Also make sure to either buy aluminum or polyurethane cradle bushings.  I hearthat is another substantial gain in handling.  Then of course there is the basic stuff such as heavy duty shocks and struts (KYB's are the best bang for the buck) And lowering springs, and polyurethane bushings.  I also think there is a heavyduty front swaybar you can buy as well.  A setup like this should make an engine swap handle awesome.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||camaro2fiero||05-26-2001||03:22 AM||||Thanks AkursedX, I will definetly be getting rear sway bar first.I think I will get aluminum cradle bushings when the time comes.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||05-26-2001||08:31 AM||||Although the WS6 suspension was a "better" suspension, it's still about 15 years old and worn out (unless you happen to have a very low mileage car).  Even if you're one of those that must stay completely stock (which you obviously aren't), you'd still have to replace shocks, struts, bushings, etc to regain any advantage the WS6 has in 1984.  Spend your money by upgrading instead of simply replacing.  <P>I don't think there's any such thing as a "heavy duty" sway bar.  Just a difference in size.  The stock bar on the front should be 7/8".  You can always upgrade that to a 1" bar (and if you want to save some cash, put the 7/8" bar on the rear--all it'll cost you is a new set of end links).<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000008||Brucepts||05-26-2001||09:14 AM||||I did the suspension upgrade a few years ago. I installed lowered springs, struts and poly bushings on the suspension. I didn't install the 1" sway bar or the rear one yet but that is coming sometime. If you drive daily on the highway I wouldn't do the poly bushings. You will feel every crack in the road. Ride quality is greatly degraded! But, the car will handle like a go-kart. And sure does make "straightening out the road" an experience!! Next upgrade is 16" Beretta rims/tires and when I pull the engine I'll add the poly to the cradle also.<P>------------------<BR>Advanced Dealer in the Great Material Continuum . . .||||reg||1
Z||000009||camaro2fiero||05-27-2001||12:00 AM||||My 84 2m4 has the WS8 suspension,or at least that is what the RPO designates.When I bought the car of my friend 2 months ago, he had replaced the struts and shocks.The car now has 34500 miles on her.I guess I need to get the sway bars.Lowering springs,but if I want the 16 inch Baretta wheels would I also need to do the brake upgrade at that time?Thanks for all the help you all have given.||||reg||6
Z||000010||Pontiaddict||05-27-2001||05:32 PM||||Brucepts, I agree with you on the poly thing, but if you read the problem Will discovered, you will see why poly in the front is a bad thing on the pre 88's. I have the Fiero store KYB suspension kit mostly iistalled, and I think that most of the roughness in the suspension is because the front lower control arms are binding. I reccomend upgranding to at least poly in the back though.<P>camaro2fiero, I'm not even sure if WS8 is a suspension code (but I did have it on my 86GT). You don't need to do the brake upgrade to use the Baretta wheels. If the shocks are a good brand name and don't feel worn out, keep them. If you shop around, you can get all the parts in the Fiero Stores suspension kit cheaper. I still suggest getting the poly bushings for the back and if the engine cradle bushings are worn out, replace them with poly too. The 85 and later engine cradle has better suspension geometry than the 84's do, if you find a good 85 - 87 (I beleive they used a different transmission mount in 87) cradle in the junkyard then pick that up too. <BR>||||reg||1
